        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        A good night routine needs only three steps in the right order: a gentle cleanse, one targeted serum on damp skin, and a moisturiser to seal it in.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            At night, cleanse gently, apply one targeted serum on damp skin, and finish with a moisturiser. Lighter, water-based products go on first and heavier creams go on last, so the cream can seal in what is underneath. Skin repairs itself at night and loses water more easily in the evening, which makes the moisturiser step worth keeping. Use one active at a time, and add anything new slowly.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Night routines get complicated quickly, with essences, serums, oils and masks stacked one on top of another. Skin does not need that much. It needs a clean surface, one product doing a specific job, and something to hold moisture in, in that order.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why night is different
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Your skin works on a daily cycle. A review of circadian rhythm and the skin reports that repair of skin cells peaks at night, even when the damage, such as UV exposure, happened earlier in the day.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31641418/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>1</sup></a>
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Water balance changes too. In a study of 16 healthy volunteers, researchers measured water loss through the skin, surface pH and skin temperature every two hours over 24 hours and found clear daily rhythms. Their results suggest that skin permeability is higher in the evening and at night than in the morning.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9424081/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>2</sup></a> In plain terms, the evening is a good time to treat your skin and a bad time to leave it without moisture.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        The order, step by step
    </h2>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Remove sunscreen and makeup, then cleanse.</strong> Use a mild cleanser and lukewarm water. If you wear heavy makeup or water-resistant sunscreen, a gentle first cleanse to remove it helps, followed by your usual wash.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Apply one targeted serum on damp skin.</strong> Use 3 to 4 drops of a serum matched to your main concern. Lighter, water-based products go before heavier ones.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Moisturise.</strong> A moisturiser is the step that seals the routine and supports the barrier.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14728698/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><sup>3</sup></a>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Add anything else last.</strong> If you use a facial oil or a spot treatment, it goes after your moisturiser, and only if your skin needs it.</li>
    </ol>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Give the serum a minute to settle before you apply moisturiser, so the layers do not roll off your face.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
